  [PASS] C5 [control] the measured cluster lensing/dynamical ratio is reproduced (L24 C5: 1.154 +/- 0.147)   (1.148 +/- 0.146 here)
  [FAIL] C6 [test] buying the cluster shear shape PRESERVES the lensing-versus-dynamics agreement (currently 1.55 sigma)   (canonical 4.5 sigma, alt 3.6 sigma -- the slip needed for the full shape fix is itself a measurable lensing/dynamics discrepancy, and it is larger than the measured one)

  C7 -- HOW MUCH OF THE SHAPE CAN BE BOUGHT INSIDE THAT BUDGET?  Scan the amplitude f of the added lensing
  phantom, cap it at the 3 sigma ceiling of the measured lensing/dynamical ratio, and report the residual
  shape error.  This is the honest maximum the freedom could deliver even if it were perfectly
  scale-selective.

      canonical: 3-sigma ceiling on the added lensing phantom f <= 0.73 (full fix needs f = 1); at f = 0.72 the residual shape error is +0.073 +/- 0.015 (4.9 sigma, from 8.8 sigma)
      alt      : 3-sigma ceiling on the added lensing phantom f <= 0.88 (full fix needs f = 1); at f = 0.86 the residual shape error is +0.036 +/- 0.008 (4.7 sigma, from 9.0 sigma)
  [FAIL] C8 [test] inside the 3-sigma lensing/dynamics budget the freedom removes the shape failure (residual under 3 sigma)   (canonical 8.8 sigma -> 4.9 sigma at f = 0.72, alt 9.0 sigma -> 4.7 sigma at f = 0.86 -- this is what the freedom would be WORTH if it could be made scale-selective; C9-C11 test whether it can)

  C9 -- THE CRUX.  The slip IS the weight (B5), so s = W(X) for a single-valued function W of some local
  invariant X.  Two things must then be true at once: W must be the required O(1e-5) at cluster radii, and
  it must be small enough at galaxy conditions to keep the deposited theory's Phi = Psi to 1e-4.  Because
  W is a FUNCTION of X, a galaxy point at the same X gets the SAME dimensionless slip -- and a galaxy's
  entire potential is four to five orders of magnitude shallower than a cluster's.  Candidate triggers:
  the acceleration g_bar/a0, the baryon density, and the local potential depth g_bar r.

